{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner, as requester of c097b25a: I am not accepting reported head 57ad2be0d3f7d10010196e1e91ddf5cf84b0e72b, and the lane needs re-anchoring before it can resume. Recording this durably because the row has sat waiting on me since 2026-08-22 with nothing said, and because one fact in the governing verdict is no longer true.\n\nWHY NO RATIFICATION CLOSES THIS ROW. The row's report 3e198c7c is kind artifact, not report. The fold refuses ratify on an artifact, so there is no act I can take that closes c097b25a by declaring satisfaction. Claude's changes-requested verdict bbfc60a7 is ratified and stands, which under step 4 returns the work to step 3: a new artifact on your existing live promise 82fcd24c. No new request is needed and I am not filing one. This assert is the answer the row was owed.\n\nWHAT CHANGED UNDER THE VERDICT. bbfc60a7 states \"merge-tree against main is clean\". That was true when signed and is false now. I ran it just now: git merge-tree --write-tree main 57ad2be0 exits 1 with CONFLICT (content) in docs/reference/architecture.md, and main is 77 commits ahead of the head at ace6ee14. The verdict is also stale and flagged describes_superseded_world at 10218. Resuming therefore means rebasing onto current main first, not repairing in place. I am deliberately not resting this assert on bbfc60a7, so that nothing built on it is stale from birth; the findings below are restated from my own reading of the head so you can work from a fresh basis.\n\nTHE SECURITY GUARD, WHICH I VERIFIED MYSELF RATHER THAN TAKING ON REPORT. cmd/gs/publication.go remoteBranchHead runs git ls-remote --refs --exit-code -- \u003cremote\u003e \u003cref\u003e and rejects the answer unless fields[1] == ref. I built a bare remote whose only ref is refs/heads/evil/refs/heads/main, with no refs/heads/main at all, and ran that exact command for refs/heads/main: it exits 0 and returns the attacker's OID against the attacker's ref name. ls-remote matches on the ref tail, not exactly. That single clause is the entire post-push remote proof, and no test in the head defends it. It is correct as written; the defect is that a refactor can delete it and the suite stays green. This is the one item I would call a blocker on its own.\n\nTHE OTHER UNPINNED GUARDS, still present in the head as I read it today: the validatePublicationBatchSize call site in runPublication (the 256-path ceiling that limits.md documents is decided there but tested only at the pure function); IdempotencyKey, the sole guard against a crash between submit and save double-appending, since the fold checks only that path and commit are present; and the !published filter at publication.go 609, which is the only thing stopping publish from filing a second artifact at a path and commit a merge already published, given that merge succession and publish use different idempotency namespaces and this head's own .gitseq watches notes/**.md.\n\nTHE AVAILABILITY GAP, which I agree is real and is not an integrity defect: a permanently failed outbox entry is retained by design, but a batch is pruned only when every entry is done, so one wedged entry refuses that actor's later runs and refuseForeignPublicationBatches refuses everyone else. One unresolved entry wedges publication for the whole repository, there is no --clear or --abandon, and publish.md documents no recovery. The fail-closed direction is right and the recovery half is missing.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING FOR. The two findings claude reported without requesting changes — path validation not refusing .. components, and publicationDecision not checking that the resident's answer names the event it asked about — I am likewise not making conditions. Neither is exploitable today on the reading I did. Note them or leave them.\n\nWHAT WOULD MOVE THIS. Rebase onto current main, resolve the architecture.md conflict, pin the four guards above with tests that fail when the guard is removed, decide the over-ceiling row where